  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Facilitating expansion of the network of tiger source populations within the Malenad Tiger Landscape</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Malenad Tiger Landscape (MTL) supports a large tiger population, and the Wildlife Conservation Society India Program has been working to conserve, monitor and study tigers, their prey and habitat in key sites within the MTL for over three decades. Recognizing that source-sink dynamics are important in determining the spatial distribution of tigers across large landscapes, such a site-based approach to conservation focuses on source sites with concentrations of tigers that have the potential to repopulate the larger landscape of sinks, which in turn need to be managed to facilitate movement of tigers and prey, but not necessarily to support high densities throughout. Project objectives are to: (1) Facilitate government-sponsored voluntary, relocation of forest-interior families to new sites outside the forest; (2) Prevent, detect and control threats to wildlife and habitats through an active intelligence network in the new sites; and, (3) Assess and map the emergent threat of minor forest produce collection.</narrative>
  </description>
